\begin_layout Section
Some equations
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Fluid-Dynamics"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bernoulli’s equation in fluid dynamics expresses the energy of incompressible
 fluids i.e., the fluid density is constant and in frictionless flow at the
 coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 Daniel Bernoulli stated, in such conditions, that the fluid pressure 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{s}$
\end_inset

, kinetic energy 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}\rho u_{s}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and potential energy 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho gz_{s}$
\end_inset

 correlates.
 Bernoulli published his principle in his book Hydrodynamic in 1738.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

In incompressible fluid conditions, the total energy equation remains constant
 according to equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:1PointFluid"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "Storck2010"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{s}+\frac{1}{2}\rho u_{s}^{2}+\rho gz_{s}=\mathrm{constant}\label{eq:1PointFluid}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Due to 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:1PointFluid"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, the energy at two points on a streamline will be the same.
 The principle can be utilized by using two points 
\begin_inset Formula $1\:\&\:2$
\end_inset

 on a streamline to extract unknown values according to the equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: 2PointFluid"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{1}+\frac{1}{2}\rho\bar{u}_{1}^{2}+\rho gz_{1}=p_{2}+\frac{1}{2}\rho\bar{u}_{2}^{2}+\rho gz_{2}\label{eq: 2PointFluid}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we assume the system is incompressible, if there is no change in potential
 energy i.e.
 the relation of two points distance to a reference level is zero and if
 no pressure drop is allowed, the kinetic energy in point 1 must be larger
 than the kinetic energy in point 2 according to the equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:BernoulliSimplified"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{2}\geq p_{1},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta\rho gz=0,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{2}-\rho_{1}=0,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{1}+\bar{u}_{1}^{2}=p_{2}+\bar{u}_{2}^{2}\label{eq:BernoulliSimplified}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By using the continuity equation of fluids, the velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{u}$
\end_inset

 of the fluid can be determined by the cross-sectional area 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 according to equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:FluidContinuity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A_{1}\bar{u}_{1}=A_{2}\bar{u}_{2}\label{eq:FluidContinuity}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To prevent a pressure drop within the system, 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{2}$
\end_inset

 must be greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{1}$
\end_inset

 to achieve a greater fluid velocity of point 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Dimensional Tolerances and Interference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A nominal dimension of a model in CAD-environment will not be fully achieved
 when manufactured.
 The absolute dimension of the manufactured model is determined by the accuracy
 of the manufacturing method.
 This must be taken into consideration when designing a component.
 The designer determines the allowed deviation of the nominal dimension.
 The acceptable deviation within the maximum and minimum dimensions is called
 tolerances.
 Different areas requires different tolerances and it’s a critical decision
 due to the tolerance accuracy directly impacts the cost.
 The cost factor elevates exponentially as the accuracy of the tolerance
 widths increases, see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:CostToleranceCausation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 Manufacturing method, tools and scrapping are a few factors that affects
 the exponentially growth of the increased cost.
\end_layout

