\begin_layout Section
Contact Mechanics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Contact mechanics studies the deformation and stresses of a solid which
 is in contact with another solid.
 The following formulation of contact mechanics applies if one solid is
 a fixed rigid body.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The conditions of a contact system are defined by Signorini's contact conditions
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "Stromberg2012"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The potential energy of the system in function of a displacement vector
 is defined by
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Pi(\boldsymbol{d})=\frac{1}{2}\boldsymbol{d}^{T}\boldsymbol{Kd}-\boldsymbol{F}^{T}\boldsymbol{d},\label{eq:PotentialEnergySignorini's}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\series bold
K
\series default
 is the stiffness matrix and 
\series bold
F
\series default
 is the external nodal force vector.
 The difference of the elastic energy within the system and the external
 work results in the potential energy within the system.
 The stage as which the lowest potential energy within the system is the
 same stage which satisfies equilibrium.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\text{\underset{\boldsymbol{d}}{\mathrm{min}}\:\Pi(\boldsymbol{d})}\label{eq:PotentialEnergyEquilibrium}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Lowest potential energy within the system is defined by
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\nabla\Pi(\boldsymbol{d})=\boldsymbol{Kd}-\boldsymbol{F}=0\label{eq:MINPotentialEnergy}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename graphics/Contact mechanics.png
	lyxscale 35
	width 40page%

\end_inset


\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Elastic body kinematic constrained by a fixed rigid obstacle.
 Adopted from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "Stromberg2012"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset

An obstacle is presented to restrict the elastic body to deform freely.
 Kinematic constraints which prevents the body to penetrate the obstacle
 are defined by
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{d}\boldsymbol{n}-g\leq0,\label{eq:KinematicConstraint}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{d}$
\end_inset

 is displacement vector, 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{n}$
\end_inset

 is normal direction vector of contact and 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 is distance to the obstacle in normal direction.
 The equation must be less or equal to zero to meet the constraints i.e.
 the distance to the obstacle must be greater or equal to the dislocation
 to have an operating contact.
 The kinematic constraints can be defined for all contacts nodes by
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{C}_{N}\boldsymbol{d}-\boldsymbol{g}\leq0,\label{eq:GLOBALKinematicConstraint}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{C}_{N}$
\end_inset

 is a transformation matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{g}$
\end_inset

 is a vector of all initial gaps 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The kinematic constraints can be included in the equilibrium principle of
 the contact problem, i.e.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\text{\begin{cases}
 \mathrm{\;\underset{\boldsymbol{d}}{min}\:}\Pi(\boldsymbol{d})\\
 \;\mathrm{s.t.}\;\boldsymbol{C}_{N}\boldsymbol{d}-\boldsymbol{g}\leq0 
\end{cases}}\label{eq:EquilibriumPrinciple}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The optimal solution of constrained optimization problems are given by Karusch-K
uhn-Tucker conditions (KKT-conditions):
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ensuremath{\begin{cases}
\boldsymbol{Kd}-\boldsymbol{F}+\boldsymbol{C}_{N}^{T}\boldsymbol{P}_{N}^{A}=0\\
\boldsymbol{P}_{N}^{A}\geq0\\
\boldsymbol{C}_{N}\boldsymbol{d}-\boldsymbol{g}\leq0\\
\boldsymbol{P}_{N}^{A}\circ\left(\boldsymbol{C}_{N}\boldsymbol{d}-\boldsymbol{g}\right)=0
\end{cases}},\label{eq:LagrangeFormulation}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{P}_{N}^{A}$
\end_inset

 is the contact force in normal direction of the contact.
 The formulation of this problem is called Lagrange formulation.
 A central distinction of Lagrangian formulation is that it treats the condition
s as additional equations and lagrange multipliers 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{P}_{N}$
\end_inset

 are interpreted as contact forces.
 The first condition of the lagrange formulation defines the equilibrium
 state of the system which is given by Signorini's contact conditions combined
 with the contact force.
 The remaining conditions are given by KKT-conditions.
 The first condition of KKT-conditions states that the contact force must
 not be negative.
 The second KKT-condition states that the contact distance must be greater
 or equal than the displacement in normal direction i.e.
 the body can not penetrate the obstacle.
 The third and last condition states that if there is no contact, the contact
 force must be zero.
 The last condition also states that if there is contact, the contact force
 must be greater than zero.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Commercial FEA softwares approaches contact problems with an augmented formulati
on of Lagrangian combined with either Uzawa's or Newton's method.
 An augmented Lagrangian formulation includes the expression of which stage
 equilibrium is satisfied and an equivalent formulation of Signorini's condition
, which is defined by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
\;\underset{P_{N}^{A}}{\mathrm{max}}\:\left(d_{N}^{A}-g^{A}\right)P_{N}^{A}\\
\;s.t.\;P_{N}^{A}\geq0
\end{cases}\label{eq:AugmentedLagrangeFormulation}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The contact force 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{N}^{A}$
\end_inset

 is equivalent to projection defined by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\text{\begin{cases}
 P_{N}^{A}=\left(P_{N}^{A}+r\left(d_{N}^{A}-g^{A}\right)\right)_{+}\\
 \;\mathrm{where}\;\mathbb{R_{+}}=\left\{  x:x\geq0\right\}  
\end{cases}}\label{eq:ContactForceProjection}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The foundation of Uzawa's and Newton's methods constitutes of the projection
 of the contact force defined in (2.14).
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "Stromberg2012"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Material Science and Engineering
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Material selection is a critical part of product development as it has a
 direct impact of the performance of the product.
 Material selection can also enable that tough requirements of the product/compo
nent is met.
 It is essential that important material properties are prioritized in order
 to optimize the function of a product or component.
 Material properties can be categorized into general, mechanical, optical,
 thermal and electrical, just to mention a few.
 Important material characteristics regarding this thesis are defined below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Engineering Stress and Strain
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Stress, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

, within the material occurs when a force, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

, is acting on the cross-sectional area, 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, according to equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Stress"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 Engineering stress does only account for the initial cross-sectional area,
 whereas the force can differentiate in a stress-strain curve.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma=\frac{F}{A}\label{eq:Stress}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Strain, 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

, is a result of an applied stress on the material.
 If the applied tensile or compressive stress is large enough, the material
 will either increase or decrease in length.
 Strain is defined by the change in length, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta l$
\end_inset

, divided by the original length, 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{0}$
\end_inset

, according to equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Strain"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\varepsilon=\frac{l_{i}-l_{0}}{l_{0}}=\frac{\Delta l}{l_{0}}\label{eq:Strain}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Hooke's Law
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Materials do not behave similarly when exposed to stress.
 A measurement of a material’s ability to withstand changes in length when
 stress is applied was there for required to form a relationship.
 Young’s modulus 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

, named after the 18th-century English scientist Thomas Young, defines such
 relationship.
 Hooke’s law defines the relationship between stress-strain-young’s modulus
 according to equations 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Young's"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "Callister2011"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E=\frac{\sigma}{\varepsilon}\label{eq:Young's}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Yield Stress
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Elastic deformation refers to the ability of a material to return to its
 original form after being exposed to stress.
 If however, the applied stress is large enough, the material will undergo
 permanent deformation, also known as plastic deformation.
 The stress limit of elastic deformation is determined by the proportional
 point 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

, which is individual for each material.
 The proportional point 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 is defined by the initial divergence from linearity of the stress-strain
 curve.
 The initial divergence is very difficult to define and as a consequence,
 yield stress/strength has been introduced.
 The yield stress of a material is defined by the offset method.
 The method constructs a parallel line to the linear stress-strain curve
 with an offset of 0.2% strain.
 The stress corresponding to where the constructed line intersects with
 the stress-strain curve is defined as the yield stress, or the yield strength
 of a material, see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:YieldStress"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 As 0.2% strain is almost negligible, the yield strength 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{y}$
\end_inset

 is often referred to the stress limit which a material can withstand without
 plastically deform.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "Callister2011"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

