\begin_layout Subsection
Strain Hardening
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Strain hardening, or work hardening, is the hardening which increases the
 strength and decreases the ductility of a material which experience strain.
 Strain hardening can be defined in an engineering stress-strain curve or
 in a true stress-strain curve.
 An engineering stress-strain curve is designated by the computation with
 a constant area.
 A constant cross-sectional area generates a curve which displays that no
 additional stress is required to fracture as the stress reached the material's
 ultimate tensile.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A true stress-strain curve defines stress and strain accordingly,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{true}=\frac{F}{A_{i}}\label{eq:TrueStress}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\varepsilon_{true}=\mathrm{ln}(\frac{L_{i}}{L_{0}})\label{eq:TrueStrain}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
True stress and strain can be defined in relation to engineering strain
 can be expressed according to the definitions 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: TrueStress2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 & 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:TrueStrain2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{true}=\sigma(1+\varepsilon)\label{eq: TrueStress2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\varepsilon_{true}=\mathrm{ln}(1+\varepsilon)\label{eq:TrueStrain2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The definition of stress and strain separates the true and engineering approach.
 True stress and true strain takes the instantaneous values of the cross-section
al area 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the length 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}$
\end_inset

 into account according to the definitions expressed in eq 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:TrueStress"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 and equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:TrueStrain"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 The engineering stress-strain only considers the original cross-sectional
 area for stress and the final strain for strain.
 The result of this is two different looking stress-strain curves whereas
 true stress-strain will be conducted in this thesis, see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:True-Stress-Strain-curve"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Strain hardening occurs as the stress exceeds the yield stress.
 The material plastically deformations at stresses higher than the material's
 yield strength.
 Plastic deformation permanently deforms the material, previously called
 strain hardening.
 The stress-strain relation of strain hardening can be displayed using a
 tangent modulus.
 Tangent modulus can be defined as bilinear or multilinear.
 A bilinear tangent modulus consists of only one linear tangent between
 two points.
 Multilinear consists of several linear tangents.
 Bilinear tangent modulus can be useful when the strain hardening behavior
 is unknown but e.g.
 yield stress and Ultimate tensile stress is known.
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To calculate the tangent modulus between the point of yield stress and the
 point of ultimate tensile stress, four coordinates are required.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{y}$
\end_inset

 yield stress, 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{y}$
\end_inset

 strain at yield stress, 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{UTS_{true}}$
\end_inset

 strain at true ultimate tensile stress and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{UTS_{true}}$
\end_inset

 true ultimate tensile stress.
 The coordinates are obtained accordingly,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sigma_{y}=\text{Given\:value\:of\:yield\:stress\:[MPa]}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\varepsilon_{y}=\frac{\sigma_{y}}{E}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\varepsilon_{UTS_{true}}=\mathrm{ln}(1+\frac{\text{elongation\%}}{100})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sigma_{UTS_{true}}=\sigma_{UTS}(1+\varepsilon_{UTS_{true}})\:\text{[MPa]},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 is the Young's modulus.
 The slope of the tangent modulus can then be calculated by equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:TangentModulusCalc"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{t}=\frac{\left(\sigma_{UTS_{true}}-\sigma_{y}\right)}{\left(\varepsilon_{UTS_{true}}-\varepsilon_{y}\right)}\label{eq:TangentModulusCalc}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


