\begin_layout Subsection
Ductility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ductile materials can deform to a certain rate without fracturing.
 Ductile materials are favorable when the design of the component requires
 forming operations.
 Ductility is defined in percent as elongation according to the equation
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Elongation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\%EL=\left(\frac{l_{f}-l_{o}}{l_{0}}\right)100\label{eq:Elongation}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The opposite of ductile is brittle.
 The atoms in brittle materials are bonded and structured in such way to
 prevent strain.
 Fracture in brittle material can there for be sudden and unexpected, see
 Figure 
